# Bobcat and Red Dimaondback Evolutionary Modeling
# Method:
#   For each evolutionary mass track:
#   1. Restrict the evolutionary track to the observed age range.
#   2. Interpolate the evolutionary track in age.
#   3. Determine whether the track reaches the observed
#      luminosity interval WHILE it is inside the allowed age interval.
#   4. A mass track is considered evolutionary-consistent
#      ONLY if:
#          age_min <= age <= age_max
#      and
#          logL_min <= logL(age) <= logL_max
#      at the SAME AGE.
#   5. The corresponding evolutionary radius is evaluated over
#      the simultaneous age + luminosity intersection.
#   6. Only evolutionary tracks that genuinely intersect
#      BOTH constraints are retained.
#   7. The overall allowed evolutionary mass and radius ranges
#      are determined from all allowed tracks.
#   8. The midpoint of the evolutionary radius range is used
#      to derive Teff from the measured bolometric luminosity:
#          L = 4 pi R^2 sigma T_eff^4
#   9. The midpoint of the evolutionary mass range and the
#      midpoint evolutionary radius are used to derive:
#          g = G M / R^2
#          logg = log10(g)
#  10. Half of the evolutionary mass/radius range is treated
#      as the uncertainty:
#          sigma_R = (Rmax - Rmin) / 2
#          sigma_M = (Mmax - Mmin) / 2
#  11. Luminosity uncertainty is propagated as an absolute
#      luminosity uncertainty derived from the measured
#      uncertainty in log(L/Lsun):
#          sigma_L = ln(10) * L * sigma_logL
#      This is then combined in quadrature with the radius
#      uncertainty when calculating Teff.
#  12. Mass and radius uncertainties are combined in quadrature
#      when calculating logg.
#   13. Produce four plots per target/model:
#          - Age vs log(L/Lsun) [FULL]
#          - Age vs log(L/Lsun) [ZOOM]
#          - Age vs Radius      [FULL]
#          - Age vs Radius      [ZOOM]

